1
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:05,982
Now the main event: the brain signal.

2
00:00:05,982 --> 00:00:10,408
Each EEG electrode measures the voltage at one spot on your scalp.

3
00:00:10,408 --> 00:00:20,805
But a voltage only means something compared with another point, so every channel is measured against a shared reference electrode, usually on your earlobe.

4
00:00:20,805 --> 00:00:23,890
That reference connects to the pin called SRB.

5
00:00:25,067 --> 00:00:28,044
The electrode wires plug into the header.

6
00:00:28,044 --> 00:00:31,821
Each channel has two pins, P and N, in the two rows.

7
00:00:31,821 --> 00:00:37,631
Then there's SRB, the reference, and BIAS, which we'll meet in the next chapter.

8
00:00:37,631 --> 00:00:43,877
At the two ends sit small sockets for the analog supplies, which the Daisy uses later.

9
00:00:45,067 --> 00:00:50,661
From each pin, the signal first meets a TVS diode, on the underside, right below the pins.

10
00:00:50,661 --> 00:00:53,396
TVS stands for transient voltage suppressor.

11
00:00:53,396 --> 00:00:55,385
Normally it does nothing at all.

12
00:00:55,385 --> 00:01:05,827
But if you touch the pins with a static charge of thousands of volts, it switches on in nanoseconds and dumps that zap to ground, before it can reach the delicate chip.

13
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:10,410
Next, through the board to the top: a 2.2 kΩ resistor.

14
00:01:10,410 --> 00:01:14,389
Four resistors are packed into each of these tiny black arrays.

15
00:01:14,389 --> 00:01:21,210
If something still gets past the diode, the resistor limits how much current can flow into the chip's input.

16
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:26,959
Then back underneath: a 1 nF capacitor from each line to ground.

17
00:01:26,959 --> 00:01:33,157
Together with the 2.2 kΩ resistor, it forms a filter with a cutoff around 72 kilohertz.

18
00:01:33,157 --> 00:01:41,207
Brain signals are below 100 hertz, so they pass untouched, but radio noise from Wi-Fi and phones is drained away.

19
00:01:41,207 --> 00:01:46,621
Notice the zig-zag: diode underneath, resistor on top, capacitor underneath.

20
00:01:46,621 --> 00:01:49,470
The signal hops through the board twice.

21
00:01:50,667 --> 00:01:53,324
And now the signal reaches the ADS1299.

22
00:01:53,324 --> 00:02:04,224
For each of the 8 channels, the chip first takes the difference between the electrode and the reference, so the hum your whole body carries largely cancels out.

23
00:02:04,224 --> 00:02:09,197
Then a programmable gain amplifier multiplies that tiny difference by 24.

24
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:12,473
Why only 24, and not 1,000?

25
00:02:12,473 --> 00:02:19,152
Because electrodes also carry a slowly drifting offset, up to a few hundred millivolts.

26
00:02:19,152 --> 00:02:27,060
With a gain of 24, the chip's input range is plus or minus 187 millivolts, wide enough for that offset.

27
00:02:27,060 --> 00:02:33,202
And its 24-bit converter is so fine that one step is only about 0.02 microvolts.

28
00:02:33,202 --> 00:02:38,960
So we get both: room for the offset, and detail far below the brain signal.

29
00:02:40,133 --> 00:02:43,560
Next comes the analog-to-digital converter.

30
00:02:43,560 --> 00:02:51,370
All 8 channels are converted at exactly the same instant, 250 times a second, into 24-bit numbers.

31
00:02:51,370 --> 00:03:05,476
The converter needs a ruler to measure against: a 4.5 volt reference made inside the chip, held steady by capacitors on its VREF and VCAP pins, including C2, the big 100 µF one.

32
00:03:05,476 --> 00:03:14,083
When a new set of 8 samples is ready, the chip pulls a pin called DRDY, data ready, to say: come and get it.

33
00:03:15,267 --> 00:03:17,553
The ADS1299 has a few more tricks.

34
00:03:17,553 --> 00:03:22,664
In front of each channel is a set of switches, called the input multiplexer.

35
00:03:22,664 --> 00:03:25,018
Normally it connects the electrode.

36
00:03:25,018 --> 00:03:39,140
But the firmware can also short a channel's inputs together to measure the chip's own noise, feed in a built-in test square wave to check everything works, or measure the chip's temperature and supply voltages.

37
00:03:39,140 --> 00:03:49,766
It also decides how the reference is used: with SRB2, the shared reference goes to each channel's P input separately, which is what the Cyton uses by default.

38
00:03:49,766 --> 00:03:52,927
With SRB1, it goes to all the N inputs at once.

39
00:03:54,133 --> 00:03:56,845
It can even check your electrodes.

40
00:03:56,845 --> 00:04:04,022
The chip sends a tiny current, 6 nanoamps, through each electrode, wobbling at 31.2 hertz.

41
00:04:04,022 --> 00:04:13,114
From the voltage that this creates, software works out how well each electrode touches your skin, using Ohm's law.

42
00:04:13,114 --> 00:04:16,783
That's the impedance check in the OpenBCI GUI.

43
00:04:16,783 --> 00:04:22,923
A good contact has low impedance; a poor one shows up as a big, noisy signal.

44
00:04:24,133 --> 00:04:31,268
Finally, look at all the small capacitors gathered around the ADS1299, many of them right underneath it.

45
00:04:31,268 --> 00:04:39,706
Every power pin of the chip gets its own: usually a 1 µF and a 0.1 µF side by side, placed as close to the pin as possible.

46
00:04:39,706 --> 00:04:43,548
The big one stores energy, the small one reacts fastest.

47
00:04:43,548 --> 00:04:51,163
Without them, the chip's own switching would make its supply bounce, and that bounce would show up in your EEG.

48
00:04:52,333 --> 00:04:53,626
Chapter recap.

49
00:04:53,626 --> 00:05:04,798
Electrode, then a TVS diode against static, a 2.2 kΩ resistor to limit current, and a 1 nF capacitor against radio noise.

50
00:05:04,798 --> 00:05:15,693
Then the ADS1299: subtract the reference, multiply by 24, convert to 24 bits, 250 times a second, and ring data ready.
